WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.667
-You look great. Thank you
so much for being here.

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:03.968
-Well, you know,
I don't have a life.

00:00:03.968 --> 00:00:06.734
[ Laughter ]

00:00:06.734 --> 00:00:09.067
So, really,
it boils down to that.

00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:11.434
And I'm like six blocks away.
-Yeah.

00:00:11.434 --> 00:00:13.434
-So this was great.
-Oh, perfect.

00:00:13.434 --> 00:00:15.200
-Yeah.
-You were telling me backstage

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.634
you got to visit your mom.
-I did.

00:00:17.634 --> 00:00:21.934
She's --
My mom is 103.

00:00:21.934 --> 00:00:23.434
-Look at this.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:23.434 --> 00:00:25.033
♪♪

00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:27.400
Aww.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.300
-And she says
she's not a day over 97.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.400
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, wow.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.000
How is she feeling?
How's she feeling?

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.934
She feels good.
-She's feeling --

00:00:35.934 --> 00:00:40.734
You know, look -- she's 103,
and it's a blessing that...

00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:41.834
-Yeah.
-...that we're --

00:00:41.834 --> 00:00:44.367
You know,
that many people say that it is,

00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:47.133
that I've gotten to spend
this much time with her.

00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:48.400
That's tremendous.
You know that.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.000
-Yeah, absolutely.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.634
Great.

00:00:53.634 --> 00:00:56.701
-But she's 103.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:56.701 --> 00:01:00.200
So sometimes she's here,
sometimes she's there.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.467
[ Laughter ]

00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:04.133
And, uh, I'm --
I'm extremely --

00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:06.467
She doesn't --
She doesn't have Alzheimer's.

00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:07.901
-No.
-No dementia.

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:09.534
-Great.
-She has something

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:12.868
that doctors haven't diagnosed.

00:01:12.868 --> 00:01:15.968
She just makes stuff up.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:15.968 --> 00:01:20.434
And, you know, it's --
it's interesting, you know?

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:22.501
Many --
You know, you got Branson

00:01:22.501 --> 00:01:25.300
and Bezos and Musk
all have spent

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:30.200
billions and billions of dollars
to get out into space, where --

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.200
where she's out there
four or five times a day.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.000
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.901
And they're -- they're
spending that kind of money.

00:01:37.901 --> 00:01:39.834
And I --
I call where she's living,

00:01:39.834 --> 00:01:42.467
her assisted living home.
It's a launch pad.

00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:44.133
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:47.868
And she's able to --
She's doing this not on --

00:01:47.868 --> 00:01:50.400
on pennies really,
'cause she's able to do this --

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.968
Her fuel is actually oatmeal.

00:01:52.968 --> 00:01:55.167
[ Laughter ]

00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:57.667
-They could save --
They could save a lot of money.

00:01:57.667 --> 00:01:59.767
-Yeah, exactly.
-It's ridiculous.

00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:02.334
-Speaking of family, recently,
I saw you on the episode

00:02:02.334 --> 00:02:03.968
of "Finding Your Roots."
-Yes.

00:02:03.968 --> 00:02:06.400
-That show. Did you enjoy it?
Did you love it?

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.300
-It was extraordinary.
I mean, it's remarkable

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:10.868
to be -- to see your --

00:02:10.868 --> 00:02:14.100
There were questions I had
that nobody in the families

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.067
seemed to have answer to
or wouldn't talk about with me,

00:02:17.067 --> 00:02:19.367
and so it was really --
And you see the pictures,

00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:22.934
and then you see, you know,
that they came to Ellis Island,

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:26.934
you see their boat passages --
I mean, it's extraordinary.

00:02:26.934 --> 00:02:28.567
It's overwhelming.
-And you found out

00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:32.367
that you're related
to Marc Maron.

00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:36.567
-Tough relationship, one angry
Jewish comic related to --

00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:39.634
Oh!
Another angry Jewish comic.

00:02:39.634 --> 00:02:41.067
[ Laughter ]
I was shock--

00:02:41.067 --> 00:02:44.601
When he said it to me,
most guys express shock.

00:02:44.601 --> 00:02:47.367
I looked at him like,
"Of course.

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:50.200
This isn't news, schmuck!"
[ Laughter ]

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.367
Meanwhile, Roy Wood Jr., who...
-Very funny.

00:02:54.367 --> 00:02:57.901
-Very funny and a colleague
on "The Daily Show,"

00:02:57.901 --> 00:03:03.267
he -- he's -- his relation
was with John Lewis!

00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:05.834
Oh, are you kidding me?!
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.834 --> 00:03:09.868
I get Maron,
he gets a civil-rights icon!

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:12.434
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.434 --> 00:03:14.801
-I know, but it's Maron.

00:03:14.801 --> 00:03:18.067
-I mean, I like Marc,
but give me a break.

00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:20.501
I want to talk to you
about your stand-up.

00:03:20.501 --> 00:03:22.968
The Off the Rails stand-up tour,
right now,

00:03:22.968 --> 00:03:25.534
tickets are available
at lewisblack.com.

00:03:25.534 --> 00:03:28.234
But this is -- you're one of
my favorite stand-ups.

00:03:28.234 --> 00:03:31.334
And then, you went through
500 days of not doing anything.

00:03:31.334 --> 00:03:33.367
You were in lockdown.
-Yeah, I was in lockdown.

00:03:33.367 --> 00:03:37.033
And here's one thing you learn
in lockdown is

00:03:37.033 --> 00:03:39.834
that solitary confinement
is a punishment.

00:03:39.834 --> 00:03:41.200
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.300
Because your brain can only
go through so many things,

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.667
and once it's finished going
through those things a lot,

00:03:45.667 --> 00:03:48.934
it comes after you!
[ Laughter ]

00:03:48.934 --> 00:03:50.667
-It's true.
-You know, and some people

00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:53.701
would do Zoom comedy and stuff.
-You didn't do that?

00:03:53.701 --> 00:03:55.167
-No!
[ Laughter ]

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:57.501
That's like a psychiatric test.

00:03:57.501 --> 00:03:59.067
You're looking
at a wall of people.

00:03:59.067 --> 00:04:01.067
It looks like you're watching
"Hollywood Squares."

00:04:01.067 --> 00:04:04.133
[ Laughter ]
I don't need that.

00:04:04.133 --> 00:04:06.467
Then they don't -- You have
no relationship with them.

00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:10.000
I've got to be in front
of people so I can yell at them!

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.801
[ Cheers and applause ]
-That's exactly right.

00:04:14.801 --> 00:04:19.767
Your special -- Your special,
"Thanks for Risking Your Life,"

00:04:19.767 --> 00:04:22.033
just got nominated for a Grammy.
Congratulations on that.

00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:26.968
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:26.968 --> 00:04:30.968
This could be your third Grammy,
by the way, for comedy.

00:04:30.968 --> 00:04:32.167
-Yeah.
-Did you ever think

00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:33.200
you'd be a multiple
Grammy winner?

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.501
-No. Who thinks that?
-Yeah.

00:04:35.501 --> 00:04:39.033
-You've got to be on egomaniac.
You got to be crazy.

00:04:39.033 --> 00:04:40.834
No, I never thought that.
-Never.

00:04:40.834 --> 00:04:42.901
-No, I didn't even think
I would be a comic.

00:04:42.901 --> 00:04:44.601
I thought I'd be teaching drama

00:04:44.601 --> 00:04:47.367
in some
sad little Southern school.

00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:48.501
[ Laughter ]
-Really?

00:04:48.501 --> 00:04:50.667
-Yeah.
-How did you come up

00:04:50.667 --> 00:04:52.567
with the title,
"Thanks for Risking Your Life"?

00:04:52.567 --> 00:04:56.300
-Well, we were on tour,
and it was the last day.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:58.801
Literally,
we were going into a lockdown.

00:04:58.801 --> 00:05:01.234
I knew we were going into
a lockdown because my friend,

00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:02.434
Kathleen Madigan,
another really great comic...

00:05:02.434 --> 00:05:05.400
-Oh, I love Kathleen Madigan!
She's great.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.467
-...and my -- and myself
had been tracking the virus.

00:05:08.467 --> 00:05:10.868
Like, we were the Fauci and Birx
of the comedy world.

00:05:10.868 --> 00:05:11.968
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.968 --> 00:05:13.901
We knew as much
as could be possible.

00:05:13.901 --> 00:05:16.501
So I knew that things
were gonna be happening.

00:05:16.501 --> 00:05:18.534
And so, they had already --

00:05:18.534 --> 00:05:21.868
Literally, they were getting
ready to lock everything up.

00:05:21.868 --> 00:05:26.434
And so, this was --
I knew this was our last show.

00:05:26.434 --> 00:05:30.834
And they almost --
We almost didn't do the show.

00:05:30.834 --> 00:05:33.167
And I knew that I'd be
going home after this show.

00:05:33.167 --> 00:05:37.834
And I thought, "Wow.
You know, all these 1,500 people

00:05:37.834 --> 00:05:39.133
in a room..."
-Yeah.

00:05:39.133 --> 00:05:42.033
-"...close together."
And I knew what that meant.

00:05:42.033 --> 00:05:44.234
And then I said, "Whew!"
I said, "What?"

00:05:44.234 --> 00:05:46.934
You know, that thing you say as
a comic, "I really killed them"?

00:05:46.934 --> 00:05:48.067
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:48.067 --> 00:05:51.000
-You know?
Well, you know, what if I did?

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.100
-Yeah! Oh, my God.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.367
-So, I was kind of freaked.
-Yeah.

00:05:57.367 --> 00:06:00.901
And we had gotten
some extra cameras and stuff

00:06:00.901 --> 00:06:03.868
to record what --
'Cause it was really --

00:06:03.868 --> 00:06:05.934
I don't think many people
were recording --

00:06:05.934 --> 00:06:09.501
It was the last performance
by a comic that was recorded

00:06:09.501 --> 00:06:11.100
on that night
before the shutdown.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:14.033
So I thought, historically --
not that it would win a Grammy

00:06:14.033 --> 00:06:16.367
just historically, people would
look at it, go,

00:06:16.367 --> 00:06:19.701
"Oh, lookit back there.
Here's a historical document,"

00:06:19.701 --> 00:06:22.534
not a Grammy nomination.
-Yeah.

00:06:22.534 --> 00:06:27.300
-You don't think that way.
So we did it, and it was --

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.934
-And I walked out on stage,
and I looked at these people,

00:06:29.934 --> 00:06:33.501
and all I could think was --
and I had no opening line.

00:06:33.501 --> 00:06:34.901
As you could tell,
a lot of this stuff

00:06:34.901 --> 00:06:36.033
that I'm saying to you,

00:06:36.033 --> 00:06:38.501
it sounds like I just
thought of it.

00:06:38.501 --> 00:06:44.000
So I walked out, and I went,
"Thanks for risking your life."

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.300
And they went nuts.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.334
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:47.334 --> 00:06:51.567
-It's perfect. And it's --
it was true, actually.

00:06:51.567 --> 00:06:53.067
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:53.067 --> 00:06:55.601
Here's Lewis Black in
"Thanks for Risking your Life."

00:06:55.601 --> 00:06:58.267
Take a look at this.
-I'm avoiding getting started

00:06:58.267 --> 00:07:01.801
'cause it's hard
to be funny now.

00:07:01.801 --> 00:07:06.300
You know -- You know why.
'Cause it's hard to be funny

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.834
when everything's
going so great.

00:07:08.834 --> 00:07:14.434
[ Laughter, cheers ]

00:07:14.434 --> 00:07:17.467
It's just hard to be funny when
everybody's so [bleep] happy.

00:07:17.467 --> 00:07:19.334
[ Laughter ]

00:07:19.334 --> 00:07:22.100
I travel everywhere,
and, you know, everywhere I go,

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:26.300
I know it seems odd, but they
are smiling ear to [bleep] ear.

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:28.000
It's like everybody's
on ecstasy.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.033
It is like wandering through
a great big Christian camp.

00:07:32.033 --> 00:07:35.167
And as a Jew, let me tell you
how [bleep] exhausting that is.

00:07:35.167 --> 00:07:37.200
[ Laughter ]

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.667
[ Cheer and applause ]
-"Thanks for Risking your Life"

00:07:39.667 --> 00:07:42.300
is available
on all digital platforms.

